# Why paint the same church thirty times? Monet's Rouen Cathedral
tags: ["monet","impressionism","light and color","series paintings","rouen cathedral"]
teaches: ["art-history/impressionism","concepts/light-as-subject","methods/serial-observation"]
thumb: "band \"Thirty times. Why?\" picture \"File:Claude Monet, Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight, 1894, NGA 46654.jpg\" colour"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What changes between two of the paintings? What was Monet really painting?"
next: {"title":"Can dots make light? Seurat's A Sunday on La Grande Jatte","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/how-painters-caught-light/09-can-dots-make-light-seurat-s-a-sunday-on.cast"}

## Rouen Cathedral: the same stone, another light
sources:
    - id: nga
      title: Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, and Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight (both 1894)
      authors: National Gallery of Art, Washington (via Google Arts & Culture)
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Both canvases dated 1894; rooms rented across from the cathedral early in 1892, return the next winter; more than 30 views, 20 shown in 1895; thick surfaces; Monet: ''What I want to reproduce is what exists between the motif and me.'''
      url: https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/rouen-cathedral-west-façade-claude-monet/xQGPN6O01y20AA
    - id: series
      title: Rouen Cathedral (Monet series)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Painted 1892–93, reworked 1894; more than thirty; twenty shown at his Paris dealer's in 1895; surfaces encrusted with paint (Tucker)
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rouen_Cathedral_(Monet_series)
    - id: levin
      title: Monet Time Series
      authors: Golan Levin, MIT Media Lab
      year: 1999
      finding: The sun rises behind the cathedral; the façade takes direct sun as it comes round in front later in the day
      url: https://acg.media.mit.edu/people/golan/rouen/Monet_Time_Series.html
    - id: artwolf
      title: Rouen Cathedral by Monet
      authors: The Art Wolf
      year: 2024
      finding: Monet worked on several canvases at once, switching to whichever matched the light; 20 shown at Durand-Ruel, May 1895
      url: https://theartwolf.com/monet/rouen-cathedral-by-monet/

    card title "Rouen Cathedral"

B: Monet painted the front of this one church more than thirty times. Why would anyone do that?
    image art cites ["nga","series"] credit "Monet, West Façade, Sunlight, 1894. NGA, Washington" x 246 y 345 width 412 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Claude_Monet%2C_Rouen_Cathedral%2C_West_Fa%C3%A7ade%2C_Sunlight%2C_1894%2C_NGA_46654.jpg/1920px-Claude_Monet%2C_Rouen_Cathedral%2C_West_Fa%C3%A7ade%2C_Sunlight%2C_1894%2C_NGA_46654.jpg look screen regions.blue_bit [0.18,0.28,0.2,0.22] regions.blue_shadow [0.255,0.2,0.07,0.6] regions.doorways [0.48,0.6,0.48,0.38] regions.left_tower [0,0,0.27,0.92] regions.lit_stone [0.03,0.17,0.43,0.4] regions.people [0.17,0.935,0.09,0.055] regions.portal_paint [0.49,0.62,0.2,0.18] regions.rose_window [0.47,0.27,0.24,0.27] regions.signature [0.74,0.958,0.25,0.04] regions.sky [0.27,0,0.6,0.2] regions.thick_paint [0.3,0.14,0.26,0.14] regions.upper_front [0.25,0.06,0.62,0.24]

A: Because the church wasn't really his subject.
    highlight art:upper_front strength soft

A: In the winter of 1892 he rented rooms right across the square from Rouen Cathedral,
    portrait monet x 720 y 360 of "Claude Monet" cameo true

A: and the next winter he came back to do it again.
    erase monet

A: This is one  (@highlight art:signature effect detail lift true@)of them, signed in 1894, now in Washington: the west front in full sun.

    camera reset true

B: It hardly looks like stone. It looks dusted with sugar.
    highlight art:upper_front strength soft lift true

A: That's the sun, risen behind the church and come round to strike its front, washing the stone almost white.
    highlight art:lit_stone lift true

A: And where it can't reac (@highlight art:blue_bit effect detail lift true@)h, the shadows aren't grey: they're blue and violet.

A: Down in the deep doorways, the shadow even glows orange.
    highlight art:doorways effect detail lift true

    camera reset true

B: Lovely. But one sunny day doesn't explain thirty canvases.
    move art by [-71,0] duration 2

A: Here is another from the same series, also in Washington:
    image west cites ["nga"] credit "Monet, West Façade, 1894. NGA, Washington" x 825 y 345 width 404 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Claude_Monet_-_Rouen_Cathedral%2C_West_Fa%C3%A7ade_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Claude_Monet_-_Rouen_Cathedral%2C_West_Fa%C3%A7ade_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.all [0,0,1,1] regions.left_tower [0,0,0.36,0.75] regions.rose_window [0.48,0.29,0.24,0.21] regions.sky [0.38,0,0.47,0.2] regions.stone [0,0.08,0.36,0.62] reveal develop

A: the same (@highlight art:left_tower west:left_tower@) front, from almost the same spot.

B: Same church? It looks rebuilt out of ice.
    highlight west:all lift true

A: Nothing was rebuilt. The sun has gone, (@highlight west:sky west:all@) the sky is a pale haze, and the same stone has turned blue and lilac.

A: Even the great rose window, dusky violet in the sun, is a deep blue hole here.
    highlight art:rose_window west:rose_window

    quiz "Which is the stone's real colour? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 500 buttons_at.y 380 voice a
        intro: So which one shows the stone's real colour?
        right: Neither, really: on these canvases the stone has no colour of its own, only the colour of the light on it at that moment.
        wrong: Ask what colour the stone would be with no light on it at all.
        * The golden one
        + Neither, really
        * The truer-to-life one
        * The bluish one

A: Monet said that what he wanted to  (@highlight art:sky west:sky strength soft lift true@)paint was what exists between the motif and himself.

B: Between him and the church... so, the air. The light hanging in front of it.
    highlight west:all strength soft lift true

    camera on ["art:lit_stone"] zoom 2.4

A: Exactly. The cathedral is the one thing that holds still, so it becomes a screen for the thing that never does.
    highlight art:lit_stone strength strong rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

B: So he was waiting for the perfect light?
    point at.ref art:sky gesture glow

A: No, every light was the right one. He worked on sever (@highlight art:lit_stone west:all lift true@)al canvases at once, switching to whichever one matched the light of the moment.

    erase west

B: Like a cook with a row of pans, dashing to whichever one is about to boil.
    move art by [71,0] duration 2

A: And look how he laid the paint on: thick, crust on crust, until the canvas is as rough as the wall it shows.
    highlight art:portal_paint effect detail

    camera reset true

A: So when he showed them in 1895, he hung twenty (@highlight art:sky art:lit_stone art:doorways lift true@) together in Paris: one front, again and again, each in another light.

    quiz "A photographer wants to do what Monet did in Rouen. Which project follows his method?" voice a
        right: One tree, one spot, all day: everything stays fixed but the light, so the light becomes the subject.
        wrong: Monet kept everything fixed except one thing.
        * Thirty trees at noon
        * One tree, thirty angles
        + One tree, one spot, all day
        * One perfect dawn shot

B: So he wasn't painting a church thirty times. He was painting thirty lights, and the church held still for them.
    highlight art:lit_stone strength soft lift true

A: Earlier painters used light to tell a story. He (@highlight art:lit_stone art:blue_shadow art:doorways lift true@)re the light is the story, and it's never the same twice.

B: Same church, thirty pictures, and not one of them is really of the church.
    highlight art:upper_front strength soft lift true

